  "Question": "The equation \\(x+\\sqrt{x-2}=4\\) has:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\) real roots",
    "B": "\\(1\\) real and \\(1\\) imaginary root",
    "C": "\\(2\\) imaginary roots",
    "D": "No roots",
    "E": "\\(1\\) real root"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The square root is real only when \\(x\\ge2\\). Since it is nonnegative, the equation also requires \\(x\\le4\\). Notice that \\(x=3\\) works:\n\\[\n3+\\sqrt{3-2}=3+1=4.\n\\]\nTo determine whether another root is possible, compare both terms with their values at \\(x=3\\).\n\nIf \\(2\\le x<3\\), then \\(\\sqrt{x-2}<1\\), so\n\\[\nx+\\sqrt{x-2}<3+1=4.\n\\]\nIf \\(3<x\\le4\\), then \\(\\sqrt{x-2}>1\\), so\n\\[\nx+\\sqrt{x-2}>3+1=4.\n\\]\nThese cases cover every other allowed value, including the endpoints. Thus \\(x=3\\) is the only real root, and the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
